le.  \n- W4: not terminal, connects with W2,W3? W2,W3,W4 is a Chow, but I have W2,W3,W4 but only one each, so need another W3 or W4 to complete.  \n- B2 or B3: I have two of each, could make pung if I draw third, but B2/B3 unshown 3 each, but one B4 is in exposed meld, so less connectivity for Chow.  \n\nBest discard: B9 because it's terminal and isolated, and discarding it moves toward All Simples (though I still have W1 terminal). After discarding B9, I have W1,W2,W3,W4,B2,B2,B3,B3,B6,B6 \u2014 can aim for sequences: W1-W2-W3, W2-W3-W4, B2-B3-B4 (already melded), B6-B7-B8 possible, or pung B6.  \n\nReason: B9 is the only terminal in my hand besides W1, but W1 can combine with W2,W3 for possible Chow. B9 has no combo. Also, B9 might be riskier to keep if I go for All Simples. Discarding B9 now is safe given B9 was discarded before.  \n\nAnswer: Play B9"}
